\section{Person identification}{

The Fullmakt service returns each person's name as a single string in
\code{name}; it does not split it into given and family names, so no
synthetic \code{person_id} is constructed. Join to \code{\link[=brreg_roles]{brreg_roles()}} on
\code{birth_date} together with \code{name} at query time if cross-referencing
to the role network is required. Role designations are returned as
English labels looked up from \link{role_types}, with the original
Norwegian code preserved in \code{role_code}; the signing-mode designations
(\code{SIGN}, \code{SIFE}, \code{SIHV}, \code{PROK}, \code{POFE}, \code{POHV}) are included in
\link{role_types} alongside the board roles. Birth dates arrive as
\code{DD.MM.YYYY} from this service and as ISO dates from the roles
endpoint; both are parsed to \code{Date}, so the two join directly.
}

\section{Standardised vs registered rule}{

\code{rule} and \code{combination_code} come from the registry's structured
combination. Where the rule has been standardised (\code{rule_status} code
\code{"RF"}) this is the registered rule. Where it has not (\code{"RI"}), the
structured combination is the statutory default ("the board jointly")
and the actually registered rule is the free text in \code{rule_text};
read \code{rule_text} in that case. \code{rule_text} may be \code{NA} when no free
text is recorded.
}

\section{Rules the service declines to resolve}{

For some entities (Equinor ASA among them) the service answers HTTP
200 with no combinations and a routine status instead: code \code{NA},
"Generelle unntak, finnes roller som har status det ikke kan foretas
maskinelt oppslag mot", meaning a rule is registered but cannot be
resolved mechanically. The typed zero-row tibble is then returned
with attributes \code{http_status = 200L}, \code{routine_status} and
\code{routine_text}, and a \code{tidybrreg_warning_unresolved} is signalled,
so the case is distinguishable from an entity with nothing
registered (no attribute, no warning).
}

